Project Archimedes

A Self-Assembling Modular Orbital Habitat Based on Autonomous Small-Satellite Swarms: Mathematical Foundations, Architecture, and Feasibility 

ouadi Maakoul 

---

Chapter 1: Introduction

The construction of permanent human habitats in low Earth orbit (LEO) has, since the inception of the space age, followed a single paradigm: fabricate large, pressure-tight modules on Earth, launch them individually on heavy-lift vehicles, and assemble them in orbit through human extravehicular activity (EVA). The International Space Station (ISS) is the crowning achievement of this approach, requiring over 40 dedicated launches and thousands of hours of astronaut labour. Yet the ISS also demonstrates the fundamental limits of the paradigm: each module is a bespoke, multi‑billion‑dollar structure whose size is capped by the payload fairing of the launch vehicle; a single launch failure can delay the entire programme by years; and reliance on EVA imposes severe scheduling and safety constraints.

At the same time, the technological landscape has changed dramatically. CubeSats and small satellites (1–12 U) now offer substantial on‑orbit capability—propulsion, precision attitude control, inter‑satellite links, and on‑board artificial intelligence—at costs that are orders of magnitude lower than traditional spacecraft. Standardised refuelling interfaces such as the Rapidly Attachable Fluid Transfer Interface (RAFTI) are poised to turn orbital propellant into a shared, transferable resource. Autonomous electron‑beam welding of structural alloys has been demonstrated in the vacuum of space. Research groups at MIT, the European Space Agency (ESA), and NASA have begun to explore the assembly of large structures from small, mass‑produced, self‑assembling components.

What has not existed until now is a unified, mathematically rigorous, and architecturally complete blueprint for a human‑rated orbital habitat assembled entirely from small satellites. This dissertation fills that gap. We present Project Archimedes, a theoretical and design framework in which a 400 m³ pressurised habitat is born in orbit from the collective behaviour of approximately 500 identical, intelligent tiles—each no larger than a 12 U CubeSat—that navigate, dock, and reconfigure themselves under the guidance of a layered artificial intelligence. No heavy‑lift vehicle, no monolithic module, and no astronaut EVA is required.

The work rests on five research objectives:

1. Develop a formal graph‑theoretic model of a habitat under construction that simultaneously captures mechanical connections, communication links, and fluid‑transfer pathways.
2. Create a co‑design methodology for optimal assignment and collision‑free trajectory generation that scales to hundreds of heterogeneous free‑flying agents.
3. Formulate the stochastic assembly process as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) and devise a decentralised planning algorithm that provides probabilistic completeness guarantees under realistic sensor and actuator degradation.
4. Derive control barrier functions (CBFs) that maintain the algebraic connectivity of the communication graph and stabilise electromagnetic docking in the thermally extreme environment of LEO.
5. Translate these mathematical results into a concrete, fully specified system architecture and demonstrate its physical, thermal, and economic feasibility through first‑principles analysis.

The dissertation is a work of pure theory and design specification. It contains no experimental data, software code, or prototype test results. Its contribution is a self‑contained, mathematically verified foundation upon which future simulation studies, hardware development, and orbital flight experiments can be built.

