Chapter 2: Literature Review

This chapter situates Project Archimedes within the existing body of work, demonstrating that each pillar of the concept has established precedent but that no prior effort has combined them into a single, closed‑form design for a self‑assembling, pressurised orbital habitat.

2.1 Modular Space Structures and the ISS Legacy
The ISS demonstrates both the power and the limitations of modular orbital assembly. Its Common Berthing Mechanism, standardised utility interfaces, and phased integration provide direct lessons for the Archimedes architecture. However, the ISS modules each mass 10–20 tonnes and require heavy‑lift vehicles (Shuttle, Proton) for delivery. Archimedes explores the opposite extreme: modules of less than 15 kg.

2.2 Self‑Assembling and Reconfigurable Space Systems
ESA’s Advanced Concepts Team has studied self‑configuring “space cubes” that use local rules to assemble into target shapes. Their work identifies a critical constraint: decentralised control fails if the communication graph becomes disconnected, motivating our emphasis on algebraic connectivity maintenance. NASA’s ARMADAS programme has demonstrated the assembly of large truss structures by inchworm‑like walking robots, proving that kilometre‑scale support structures can be built from small voxels, though ARMADAS does not address pressurised volumes or fluid handling.

2.3 TESSERAE and Flat‑Pack Habitat Concepts
The TESSERAE (Tessellated Electromagnetic Space Structures for the Exploration of Reconfigurable, Adaptive Environments) project at MIT and the Aurelia Institute is the closest existing concept to Archimedes. TESSERAE tiles are flat‑packed, self‑assembling hexagons or pentagons that use electromagnets to dock and form geodesic shells. However, TESSERAE researchers explicitly state that their tiles are “not yet airtight, and therefore not human‑ready”, and that “precision movement in space typically requires a propulsion system”. Archimedes directly addresses both gaps.

2.4 Swarm Robotics and Distributed Assembly
The distributed robotics literature provides algorithms for assignment and formation control that scale to hundreds of agents. The distributed auction algorithm with barrier‑augmented costs and sequential convex programming (SCP) for trajectory generation has been validated on heterogeneous swarms. Archimedes unifies these into a co‑design loop that solves the assignment‑and‑motion problem simultaneously, a first for space assembly.

2.5 In‑Orbit Servicing, Refuelling, and Standardised Interfaces
Orbit Fab’s RAFTI interface and the US Space Force’s Tetra‑5/6 missions are creating a new paradigm of on‑orbit refuelling. Archimedes makes RAFTI universal across all tiles, transforming the swarm into a distributed propellant network whose survivability can be formally analysed as a flow network.

2.6 Autonomous Welding and In‑Space Manufacturing
ThinkOrbital’s 2024 orbital electron‑beam (EB) weld demonstration proved that high‑quality structural joints can be created autonomously in vacuum. We specify a dedicated welding E‑Walker that uses fiducial‑based alignment to achieve the ±2 mm positioning accuracy required for the demonstrated joint stiffness improvement of ~10³.

2.7 Artificial Intelligence for Space Mission Planning
Large language models (LLMs) are being explored for mission planning, but ESA’s ACT cautions that raw LLM outputs require formal verification. Archimedes’ “Architect” AI follows this principle: every LLM‑generated plan is verified by the SPIN model checker before execution, and a runtime monitor can revert to a pre‑verified graph‑grammar scaffold.

2.8 Identified Gaps and Contributions of This Work
No existing work simultaneously addresses: pressurised human‑ready volume from sub‑15 kg tiles; thermal‑aware electromagnetic docking; POMDP‑based planning with eclipse‑induced sensor dropout; distributed auction with algebraic connectivity penalties; and a closed economic model. Project Archimedes closes these gaps.

