Chapter 4: Architectural and System Design

This chapter translates the mathematical framework into a concrete engineering specification.

4.1 System Hierarchy: Architect, Foreman, Mason

The AI is layered in three tiers, analogous to a terrestrial construction project.

Level 1 – Architect (Strategic AI): A large language model (fine‑tuned Llama‑3) hosted on the Nucleus module. It receives high‑level human goals (“assemble a 400 m³ ring”) and decomposes them into a sequence of partial target graphs $\mathcal{G}_0^* \subset \mathcal{G}_1^* \subset \dots \subset \mathcal{G}_{\text{final}}^*$ using retrieval‑augmented generation over an engineering constraint database. Every output plan is verified by the SPIN model checker for deadlock freedom, collision avoidance, power‑loop invariants, and the safety condition $\lambda_2 \ge \lambda_{\min}$. A runtime monitor on the Foreman can revert to a pre‑verified graph‑grammar scaffold if the Architect’s plan ever violates invariants.

Level 2 – Foreman (Orchestration AI): Runs on the Nucleus. It executes the auction‑plus‑SCP assignment every 60 seconds, maintains a digital twin of the swarm, updates the POMDP belief state, and replans on any tile failure. All issued commands are local and unambiguous.

Level 3 – Mason (Edge Autonomy): Each tile runs a lightweight policy network distilled from the Foreman. It performs visual/UWB‑servoing, 6‑DOF EM dipole control, thruster allocation, and local CBF‑QP execution at 10 Hz. If communication with the Foreman is lost for more than 300 s, the Mason enters a safe‑hold mode.

4.2 Building Block Specifications

A‑Tile v0.2

· Form factor: Deployed—regular hexagon, 1.2 m flat‑to‑flat. Stowed—12 U (240 × 240 × 340 mm). Mass: 12.5 kg.
· Structure: Injection‑moulded carbon‑fibre‑reinforced polymer (CFRP), embedded magnetorquer coils. Six faces carry hybrid permanent‑magnet (NdFeB) + trim‑coil pads, with mechanical latches.
· Propulsion: 8 × 0.1 N cold‑gas (R236FA) thrusters, total Δv 18 m/s; 3‑axis reaction wheels (0.02 N·m·s).
· Sensors: Star tracker, 4 × docking cameras with AprilTag detection, 6 × time‑of‑flight proximity sensors, 6 × ultra‑wideband (UWB) ranging transceivers, MEMS IMU.
· Power: Deployable GaAs solar petals (24 W orbit‑average), 30 Wh Li‑ion battery. Qi wireless power (5 W) and galvanic connection via RAFTI when docked.
· Data: Optical inter‑tile link (1 Gbps), S‑band backup (100 kbps).
· Compute: TMR FPGA + dual‑core ARM Cortex‑R5 @ 400 MHz, 2 TOPS NPU, latch‑up protection.
· Interfaces: RAFTI port on every tile for propellant and power sharing; gender‑neutral mechanical capture.

Truss Node and Strut

· Node: 4 kg cubic, six tile‑compatible docking faces, internal bus.
· Strut: telescopic CFRP, 2 m extended, 2 kg, with 10× bending stiffness advantage over tile‑only frame.

Propellant Tank Module (PTM)

· 12 U, 22 kg wet (10 kg ASCENT propellant), tile‑grade GNC, RAFTI port.

Keeper Scaffold and Welding E‑Walker

· Scaffold: Inflatable Kevlar mesh net, 50 m diameter, 5 kg. Deployed on first launch to physically constrain the first 60 tiles within EM capture range. Crucially, the scaffold can carry distributed electromagnets to provide a background dipole field, reducing the required tile pad moment for initial capture by a factor of ~5 (see Chapter 5.5). After frame closure, the scaffold is jettisoned and de‑orbited.
· Welding E‑Walker: 12 U cube with an EB welding head (ThinkOrbital‑heritage) and a short‑range robotic arm. Truss nodes carry retro‑reflective fiducials for ±2 mm alignment.

4.3 Assembly Phases – No Human EVA

All phases are fully autonomous.

1. Nucleus + Scaffold (T0). Dedicated Falcon 9 places the Nucleus and folded scaffold into 500 km SSO. Scaffold inflates to 50 m.
2. Swarm Injection 1 (T+2w). 60 tiles + 20 nodes/struts deployed by Transporter rideshare 20 km trailing. Tiles close in 14 days using drag/cold‑gas, entering scaffold volume.
3. Frame Closure (T+8w). Foreman assigns a 2‑ring hexagonal truss. Tiles perform hierarchically clustered consensus to reach positions inside the scaffold, magnetically dock, and engage latches. The welding E‑Walker stiffens critical joints.
4. Airtight Volume Realisation (T+24w). One of two paths (see 4.4) executed to create pressure shell.
5. Utility Integration (T+48w). Additional tiles (solar, radiator, PTMs) dock autonomously.
6. Crew‑Ready (T+72w). Life support integrated and tested. Habitat pressurised.

4.4 Airtightness Strategy: Two Alternate Paths

Because TESSERAE‑style tiles are not inherently airtight, we develop two concurrent paths, with a down‑select criterion after 24‑month thermal‑vacuum and MMOD ground testing.

