Appendix B: Architecture and Design Drawings (Text Descriptions)

This appendix contains detailed textual descriptions of the architectural schematics that would normally appear as figures. Each description is intended to be directly translatable into a CAD or block diagram.

B.1 System Architecture Block Diagram

The diagram consists of five horizontal layers.

· Top layer: “Orbital Environment” (500 km SSO, β ≤ 52°, eclipse 35 min). Arrows indicate solar flux and MMOD flux.
· Second layer: “Constituent Swarm”. Shows three main groups: A‑Tiles (480), Truss Nodes/Struts (120/200), Propellant Tank Modules (20). Each group is linked to a RAFTI‑enabled fluid network.
· Third layer: “AI Hierarchy”. A large box labeled “Nucleus” contains the Architect (LLM + SPIN verifier) and Foreman (Auction + SCP + Digital Twin). Below, each tile is represented as a small “Mason” block with local CBF‑QP, visual servoing, and EM control.
· Fourth layer: “Communication Network”. Three sub‑networks: Intra‑swarm optical mesh (blue), S‑band ground link (green), UWB ranging mesh (orange). All are 2‑edge connected at all times.
· Bottom layer: “Ground Segment”. Mission control, human supervisors, and offline training infrastructure for the LLM and RL policies.

B.2 A‑Tile Exploded View

An isometric exploded view of a single A‑Tile shows:

1. Central CFRP frame – hexagonal, hollow centre for electronics stack.
2. Six docking faces – each with a circular hybrid NdFeB + trim‑coil magnet pad (grey), surrounded by three mechanical alignment cones and one central RAFTI port (silver).
3. Propulsion module – mounted on the rear face: 8 cold‑gas nozzles arranged in four pairs, a single R236FA propellant tank (spherical, 1.2 L), and associated valves.
4. Avionics stack – from top to bottom: FPGA + ARM board, NPU module, star tracker optic, 4× camera lenses pointing through face apertures, MEMS IMU, UWB antennas.
5. Power subsystem – three deployable GaAs solar panels (one per 120° segment) stowed flat against three alternate faces, a 30 Wh Li‑ion battery pack, and Qi coils behind each docking face.
6. Thermal hardware – patch heaters on battery and propellant tank, radiator strips on non‑docking faces.

B.3 Truss Node and Strut Assembly

· Truss Node: A 200 mm cube with six female docking interfaces (compatible with A‑Tile male cones). Inside: power bus bars and optical data router. Four faces include threaded inserts for strut attachment.
· Strut: A telescoping CFRP tube, collapsed length 1.2 m, extended 2.0 m. Ends have bayonet locks that engage with node inserts. A single‑use actuator (wax motor) extends the strut; once locked, the joint provides a bending stiffness of 10⁴ N·m/rad.
· Assembly sequence: A tile docks to one node, a strut is placed between two nodes by the E‑Walker, then the strut extends and locks, rigidising the truss.

B.4 Keeper Scaffold and Electromagnetic Field Layout

The scaffold is an inflated Kevlar torus of 50 m major diameter, with a net of 1 m mesh connecting it. Along the torus and at 20 distributed nodes are flat electromagnet coils (1 m² each, 100 turns, 1 A) powered by thin‑film solar cells laminated on the torus surface. The coils are oriented to produce a quasi‑uniform dipole field inside the volume (orientation perpendicular to the orbit plane). The field strength at the centre is ~10⁻⁴ T, dropping to ~10⁻⁵ T at 25 m. A‑Tile permanent magnets experience a force of ~0.2 N at 0.35 m from the net, sufficient for initial capture before final latch.

B.5 Assembly Phases CONOPS Flowchart

A vertical timeline with seven phases:

· T0: “Nucleus + Scaffold Launch”. Nucleus deploys its solar arrays; scaffold inflates.
· T+2w: “Swarm 1 Deployment”. 60 tiles + 20 nodes dispensed from Transporter; drift to scaffold over 14 days.
· T+8w: “Frame Closure”. Auction/SCP assigns tiles to ring. Tiles dock. E‑Walker welds nodes.
· T+24w: “Pressure Shell Realisation”. Bladder (Path B) inserted and inflated; or tiles engage gaskets (Path A). Pressure test.
· T+48w: “Utility Integration”. Additional solar/radiator tiles and PTMs launched every 6 weeks; self‑docking.
· T+72w: “Life Support Installation”. ECLSS racks delivered in visit vehicle, robotically installed.
· T+80w: “Crew Ingress”. First crew arrival; habitat fully operational.

B.6 Thermal Control System Schematic

The habitat thermal control uses a dual‑loop architecture:

· Internal loop: Air/water heat exchangers inside the habitat collect heat from crew and equipment. Coolant (water‑glycol) circulates through insulated pipes to heat exchangers mounted on the interior face of the tile shell.
· External loop: A network of “radiator tiles” (A‑Tiles with extended CFRP radiators, no internal volume) are docked to the exterior. Heat pipes transfer heat from the shell to the radiators. Loop flow is controlled by a central thermal management computer; pumps are distributed.
· Cold plates on the battery packs and NPUs are connected to the external loop. Multilayer insulation (MLI) covers all non‑radiating surfaces. The β‑angle constraint ensures radiators always see deep space for half the orbit.

B.7 Power Distribution and Data Network Topology

· Power: 300 solar tiles generate 60 W each peak (18 kW total). Each tile contains a maximum power point tracker (MPPT) and a 28 V regulated bus. Power is shared via the RAFTI galvanic connection and Qi wireless pads. The network is arranged as a mesh: any tile can receive power from any neighbour. The central bus is regulated at 28 V ± 2 V. During eclipse, batteries supply housekeeping loads. A central power controller (on Nucleus) balances generation and demand.
· Data: The primary intra‑swarm network is a 1 Gbps optical wireless mesh using focused LEDs and photodiodes on each docking face. A time‑division multiple access (TDMA) schedule is computed by the Foreman and distributed every second. Backup is UWB at 1 Mbps for telemetry and commands. Ground link is S‑band, 100 Mbps down, 10 kbps up, using a phased‑array antenna on the habitat.

